    "result_text": "The full test suite is green \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the project, and it passes along with a successful build.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `namesakeCount` feature on owner creation. On `POST /api/owners`, the new owner now records how many existing owners already share the same first and last name (case-insensitively) *before* the create, and that value is returned on subsequent `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` (int32, min 0) property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it (and MapStruct auto-maps it by name).\n\n2. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `namesake_count` column field with getter/setter.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 in `addOwner`, compute the count via a new `countNamesakes(firstName, lastName)` helper (streams `findAllOwners()`, matching both names with `equalsIgnoreCase`) and set it on the owner before saving \u2014 consistent with how `customerCode`/`householdId` are derived there.\n\n4. **Schema files** (`hsqldb`, `h2`, `mysql`, `postgres` `schema.sql`) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column.\n\n5. **`db/hsqldb/data.sql`** \u2014 the seed owner INSERTs use the column-less `VALUES` form, so I added the extra trailing `NULL` to each to match the new column count (the h2/postgres seeds list columns explicitly, so they needed no change).\n\nThe test `coreCountsNamesakes` creates two owners with the same first/last name (different postcodes to avoid the household-duplicate 409) and asserts the second owner's `namesakeCount` is `1` \u2014 now passing.",
